WEBVTT

00:00.290 --> 00:02.640
- [Instructor] So the goal
of this video is to prove

00:02.640 --> 00:06.640
that the three medians of
a triangle always intersect

00:06.640 --> 00:09.680
at one point, which is pretty interesting,

00:09.680 --> 00:11.650
because you would expect
two different lines

00:11.650 --> 00:13.860
with different slopes to
intersect in one point,

00:13.860 --> 00:17.740
but three lines intersecting
in one point is pretty neat.

00:17.740 --> 00:19.940
And this is true for all triangles.

00:19.940 --> 00:21.690
And so to setup this proof,

00:21.690 --> 00:23.800
I put an arbitrary triangle here,

00:23.800 --> 00:25.800
but I put one vertex at the origin.

00:25.800 --> 00:27.160
That'll simplify the math.

00:27.160 --> 00:30.480
And then I put another
vertex on the x-axis.

00:30.480 --> 00:32.030
And I've given them coordinates.

00:32.030 --> 00:34.530
So this one right over
here is at zero, zero.

00:34.530 --> 00:37.110
This one over here, we're just
saying that the x-coordinate

00:37.110 --> 00:40.380
is a, and so it's a comma zero.

00:40.380 --> 00:43.910
And then this one up here has
some x and some y-coordinate.

00:43.910 --> 00:45.950
We're just calling them b and c.

00:45.950 --> 00:48.210
This is some arbitrary triangle.

00:48.210 --> 00:50.210
And if you had some other triangle

00:50.210 --> 00:52.620
that had the same dimensions as this one,

00:52.620 --> 00:54.030
and this one can have any dimensions

00:54.030 --> 00:56.380
'cause we haven't defined a, b, and c.

00:56.380 --> 00:59.200
You could go from this triangle
to any of those other ones

00:59.200 --> 01:00.700
using rigid transformations.

01:00.700 --> 01:04.260
So if we can prove that the
median of this triangle,

01:04.260 --> 01:08.700
this general triangle, always
intersect at one point,

01:08.700 --> 01:11.730
this will be true for all triangles.

01:11.730 --> 01:13.450
So let's do a little bit more.

01:13.450 --> 01:15.050
Let's draw the medians.

01:15.050 --> 01:16.870
So what we're going to do is draw lines

01:16.870 --> 01:19.430
from each of the vertex to the midpoint

01:19.430 --> 01:21.260
of the opposite side.

01:21.260 --> 01:23.420
So if we do that, we've
drawn all the medians.

01:23.420 --> 01:26.320
And it for sure looks like
they intersect in one point.

01:26.320 --> 01:28.140
But to prove that, let's think about

01:28.140 --> 01:30.030
what the coordinates are of the midpoints

01:30.030 --> 01:31.740
of each of these sides.

01:31.740 --> 01:35.470
So what is the coordinate right over here?

01:35.470 --> 01:37.393
Pause this video and think about that.

01:38.890 --> 01:41.800
Well this is going to be the
midpoint of this top point

01:41.800 --> 01:43.100
and this bottom right point.

01:43.100 --> 01:45.280
So this length is equal to that length.

01:45.280 --> 01:48.200
And for the midpoint, you can
really just think about it

01:48.200 --> 01:51.050
as you're taking the average
of each of the coordinates.

01:51.050 --> 01:53.080
So the x-coordinate here's
going to be the average

01:53.080 --> 01:54.610
of b and a.

01:54.610 --> 01:59.403
So you could just write
that as a plus b over two,

02:00.800 --> 02:02.840
and then the y-coordinate
is going to be the average

02:02.840 --> 02:04.030
of c and zero.

02:04.030 --> 02:08.730
That would be c plus zero
over two, or just c over two.

02:08.730 --> 02:11.590
And we could do that for
each of these points.

02:11.590 --> 02:13.580
So this point right over here,

02:13.580 --> 02:18.280
its x-coordinate is going to
be the average of zero and a.

02:18.280 --> 02:20.780
So that's just a over two.

02:20.780 --> 02:22.410
And its y-coordinate is
going to be the average

02:22.410 --> 02:23.250
of zero and zero.

02:23.250 --> 02:25.200
You can see that it sits on the x-axis,

02:25.200 --> 02:27.310
so its y-coordinate is zero.

02:27.310 --> 02:29.590
And then last but not least,

02:29.590 --> 02:30.810
what's the coordinate of this point?

02:30.810 --> 02:33.530
Pause the video and
try to figure that out.

02:33.530 --> 02:35.820
All right, well it's going be x-coordinate

02:35.820 --> 02:38.490
who's going to be the
average of b and zero,

02:38.490 --> 02:40.600
which is just going to be b over two.

02:40.600 --> 02:42.780
And then the y-coordinate
is going to be the average

02:42.780 --> 02:47.500
of c and zero, which is
just going to be c over two.

02:47.500 --> 02:50.460
So way that I'm gonna prove
that all three of these medians

02:50.460 --> 02:54.780
intersect at a unique point,
is by showing you a coordinate

02:54.780 --> 02:56.770
that sits on all three lines.

02:56.770 --> 02:58.290
If it sits on all three lines,

02:58.290 --> 03:01.320
that must be the point of intersection.

03:01.320 --> 03:05.420
And that interesting
point is 2/3 along the way

03:05.420 --> 03:07.380
of any one of the medians.

03:07.380 --> 03:09.670
So one way to think
about it is the distance

03:09.670 --> 03:14.670
between the vertex and that
point is 2/3 of the length

03:15.120 --> 03:16.680
of the median.

03:16.680 --> 03:18.940
So if we just look at this blue median,

03:18.940 --> 03:21.970
the coordinate of this point
that is twice as far away

03:21.970 --> 03:25.780
from the vertex as it is
from the opposite side,

03:25.780 --> 03:27.630
it will be based on a weighted average

03:27.630 --> 03:29.570
of the x and y-coordinates.

03:29.570 --> 03:32.490
When we did a midpoint and
things were equally far away,

03:32.490 --> 03:34.730
you equally weighted the coordinates.

03:34.730 --> 03:36.950
So you just took their average.

03:36.950 --> 03:39.930
But when you're, if you
are closer to this side,

03:39.930 --> 03:42.600
you will take a weighted
average accordingly.

03:42.600 --> 03:47.600
So it's going to be 2/3
times a plus b over two,

03:48.120 --> 03:53.120
plus 1/3 times zero, and
then the y-coordinate

03:53.350 --> 03:55.863
is going to be 2/3 times c over two,

03:57.920 --> 04:00.590
plus 1/3 times the y-coordinate here,

04:00.590 --> 04:02.500
which is just going to be zero.

04:02.500 --> 04:04.410
Now once again, why do we have this 2/3

04:04.410 --> 04:05.750
and this 1/3 weighting?

04:05.750 --> 04:08.450
Because we are twice
as close to this point

04:08.450 --> 04:11.100
as we are to that point.

04:11.100 --> 04:14.930
Now if we wanted to simplify
it, what would we get?

04:14.930 --> 04:17.760
So this two would cancel with that two.

04:17.760 --> 04:22.597
So and this is zero, so we
would get a plus b over three

04:24.550 --> 04:25.630
for the x-coordinate.

04:25.630 --> 04:27.780
And for the y-coordinate, this is zero.

04:27.780 --> 04:32.210
That two cancels with that
two, and we get c over three.

04:32.210 --> 04:35.750
So we just found a point
that for sure sits on

04:35.750 --> 04:38.050
this blue median.

04:38.050 --> 04:39.570
Now let's do a similar exercise

04:39.570 --> 04:43.540
with this pink colored median.

04:43.540 --> 04:46.050
So that pink colored median,
what is the coordinate

04:46.050 --> 04:48.320
of the point that sits on that median,

04:48.320 --> 04:50.480
that is twice as far from the vertex

04:50.480 --> 04:52.740
as it is from the opposite side?

04:52.740 --> 04:55.510
Well it would be the exact same exercise.

04:55.510 --> 04:58.130
We would doubly weight these coordinates.

04:58.130 --> 05:03.130
So the x-coordinate would
be 2/3 times b over two

05:04.930 --> 05:09.930
plus 1/3 times a, 1/3 times a.

05:10.030 --> 05:13.740
And then the y-coordinate
would be 2/3 times c over two.

05:13.740 --> 05:18.540
2/3 times c over two plus 1/3 times zero.

05:18.540 --> 05:21.230
1/3 times zero.

05:21.230 --> 05:22.680
And what does that get us?

05:22.680 --> 05:23.513
Well let's see.

05:23.513 --> 05:25.360
This two cancels out with that two

05:25.360 --> 05:30.130
and we are left with b over
three plus a over three.

05:30.130 --> 05:33.470
So that's the same thing
as a plus b over three

05:33.470 --> 05:36.450
and over here, that's
zero, that cancels out,

05:36.450 --> 05:38.420
that is c over three.

05:38.420 --> 05:41.700
So notice, this exact coordinate sits on

05:41.700 --> 05:45.130
both the blue median and this pink median.

05:45.130 --> 05:48.100
So that must be the place
that they intersect.

05:48.100 --> 05:51.240
Let's see if that's also
true for this orange median.

05:51.240 --> 05:53.750
So in the orange median,
same exact exercise.

05:53.750 --> 05:55.350
And I encourage you to pause this video

05:55.350 --> 05:58.250
and try to calculate the value
of this point on your own.

05:58.250 --> 06:01.200
What's the coordinate of that
point on the orange line,

06:01.200 --> 06:05.930
where this distance is twice
as large as this distance?

06:05.930 --> 06:07.710
Well, same idea.

06:07.710 --> 06:10.660
We would doubly weight these
points right over here.

06:10.660 --> 06:15.660
So the x-coordinate would
be 2/3 times a over two,

06:15.790 --> 06:19.160
plus 1/3 times b.

06:19.160 --> 06:22.250
The y-coordinate would be 2/3 times zero.

06:22.250 --> 06:26.540
2/3 times zero plus 1/3 times c.

06:26.540 --> 06:30.150
1/3 times c, and what does that get us?

06:30.150 --> 06:31.880
Let's see, that cancels with that.

06:31.880 --> 06:34.510
So we have a over three plus b over three.

06:34.510 --> 06:38.800
Well that's the same thing
as a plus b over three.

06:38.800 --> 06:40.710
And then over here, that's just zero.

06:40.710 --> 06:44.330
And we're left with c over three.

06:44.330 --> 06:48.130
So notice, we have just shown
that this exact coordinate

06:48.130 --> 06:51.360
sits on all three medians.

06:51.360 --> 06:55.210
And so therefore, all three
medians must intersect

06:55.210 --> 06:57.990
at that point because that
point exists on all the lines.

06:57.990 --> 06:59.940
We've just shown that.

06:59.940 --> 07:02.440
And that's true for
this arbitrary triangle.

07:02.440 --> 07:04.740
You could make this triangle
have arbitrary dimensions

07:04.740 --> 07:08.340
by changing your value for a, b, or c.

07:08.340 --> 07:10.470
And if you see a triangle
that has the same dimensions

07:10.470 --> 07:13.420
but just, it's shifted or it's
in a different orientation,

07:13.420 --> 07:16.210
you can do a rigid transformation,
which doesn't change

07:16.210 --> 07:17.470
any of the dimensions.

07:17.470 --> 07:19.380
And you can show that that would be true

07:19.380 --> 07:20.973
for that triangle as well.
